660185e6\ntype: uses\n---\nhgraph/edges/43db58dcbe19__6bfa9e5202e2.md\n---\ngenerated: blueprint\nhard: true\nsource: 43db58dcbe19\ntarget: 6bfa9e5202e2\ntype: uses\n---\nhgraph/edges/f8e939ab3600__6cab7e9922e9.md\n---\ngenerated: blueprint\nhard: true\nsource: f8e939ab3600\ntarget: 6cab7e9922e9\ntype: uses\n---\nhgraph/edges/f79c4ff15e26__41df8bdbc790.md\n---\ngenerated: blueprint\nhard: true\nsource: f79c4ff15e26\ntarget: 41df8bdbc790\ntype: uses\n---\nhgraph/edges/d31f4f511cf4__205f9692b4a5.md\n---\ngenerated: blueprint\nhard: true\nsource: d31f4f511cf4\ntarget: 205f9692b4a5\ntype: uses\n---\nhgraph/edges/a9c75e1b4136__39aa86683ede.md\n---\ngenerated: blueprint\nhard: true\nsource: a9c75e1b4136\ntarget: 39aa86683ede\ntype: uses\n---\nhgraph/edges/28309e339e61__9dea41955fbb.md\n---\ngenerated: blueprint\nhard: true\nsource: 28309e339e61\ntarget: 9dea41955fbb\ntype: uses\n---\nhgraph/edg…47528 tokens truncated…orsion proposition (`mumford-frag-torsion`).'\nfile: MumfordLib/Analytic.lean\ngenerated: lean\nlean_status: lean_ok\ntitle: Mumford.ProductTorus\ntype: lean\nupdated: '2026-08-27T17:10:14'\n---\nabbrev ProductTorus (d : Type*) := UnitAddTorus dhgraph/nodes/9f923acc1f29.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Theorem of Tate (C. P. Ramanujam)\ncontent_type: proposition\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-prop-closed-subvariety-descent-finite-field\nlean_status: empty\norder: 195\nref: mumford-abelian-varieties:page-0265\ntitle: Descent of closed abelian subvarieties\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nIf $Y$ is an abelian subvariety of an abelian variety $X$ over a finite field\n$k_0$ and is $k_0$-closed, then $Y$ is an abelian variety defined over $k_0$.\nMoreover, there is a $k_0$-defined abelian subvariety $Z$ with $Y+Z=X$ and\n$Y\\cap Z$ finite. Consequently every $k_0$-abelian variety is isogenous to a\nproduct of $k_0$-simple abelian varieties, and if\n$X\\sim\\prod_iX_i^{n_i}$ with pairwise non-isogenous simple $X_i$, then\n\\[\n {\\rm End}_{k_0}^{0}X\\simeq\\prod_iM_{n_i}(D_i),\n \\qquad D_i={\\rm End}_{k_0}^{0}(X_i).\n\\]hgraph/nodes/ed5f5726c302.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Varieties\ncontent_type: lemma\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-lem-rigidity-form-I\nlean_status: empty\norder: 37\nref: M:ch2:II.4\ntitle: Rigidity Lemma, Form I\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nLet $X$ be complete, $Y,Z$ varieties, and $f:X\\times Y\\to Z$ a morphism such\nthat $f(X\\times\\{y_0\\})=\\{z_0\\}$. Then $f=g\\circ p_2$ for a morphism\n$g:Y\\to Z$.hgraph/nodes/80eb6e913ecf.md\n---\nauthor: sync\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-28T04:38:29'\ndecl: Mumford.productTorus_zsmul_torsionSubgroup_carrier_finite\ndocstring: 'The nonzero-integer torsion subgroup has a finite carrier on a finite\n\n  product torus.'\nfile: MumfordLib/Analytic.lean\ngenerated: lean\nlean_status: lean_ok\ntitle: Mumford.productTorus_zsmul_torsionSubgroup_carrier_finite\ntype: lean\nupdated: '2026-08-28T04:38:29'\n---\ntheorem productTorus_zsmul_torsionSubgroup_carrier_finite\n    {d : Type*} [Finite d] {n : ℤ} (hn : n ≠ 0) :\n    ((productTorus_zsmul_torsionSubgroup d n : AddSubgroup (ProductTorus d)) :\n      Set (ProductTorus d)).Finite := by\n  simpa [productTorus_zsmul_torsionSubgroup] using\n    (productTorus_zsmul_torsion_finite (d := d) hn)hgraph/nodes/1ff795d3956e.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Varieties\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-thm-unital-composition\nlean_status: empty\norder: 41\nref: M:ch2:II.4\ntitle: Recognition criterion\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nLet $X$ be complete, $e\\in X$, and $m:X\\times X\\to X$ satisfy\n$m(x,e)=m(e,x)=x$. Then $m$ is an abelian-variety group law with identity $e$.hgraph/nodes/256d43be1f76.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: $\\Hom(X,\\Adual)$ and the $\\ell$-adic Representation\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-thm-tate-centralizer\nlean_status: empty\norder: 161\nref: mumford-abelian-varieties:page-0232\ntitle: Tate centralizer theorem\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nIf $X$ is defined over a finite field $k_0$, with Frobenius $\\pi$, then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{End}(X,k_0)\\otimes\\mathbb Q_l\n=\\operatorname{Cent}_{\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb Q_l}(T_lX)}(T_l\\pi).\n\\]hgraph/nodes/79ab4304b44b.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: $\\Hom(X,\\Adual)$ and the $\\ell$-adic Representation\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-thm-degree-tate-determinant\nlean_status: empty\norder: 135\nref: M:ch4:IV.19\ntitle: Degree and Tate determinant\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nFor $f\\in\\operatorname{End}X$ and $l\\ne\\operatorname{char}k$,\n\\[\n\\deg f=\\det T_l(f).\n\\]\nThus $\\deg(n_X-f)=P(n)$, where $P(t)=\\det(t-T_l(f))$ is monic of degree\n$2g$ with integral rational coefficients and $P(f)=0$.hgraph/nodes/5b802ed9b979.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Varieties\ncontent_type: corollary\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-thm-base-change-criterion\nlean_status: empty\norder: 48\nref: M:ch2:II.5\ntitle: Base-change criterion\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nIf $Y$ is reduced and connected, constancy of\n$y\\mapsto\\dim H^p(X_y,\\mathcal F_y)$ is equivalent to $R^pf_*\\mathcal F$\nbeing locally free and having fibers $H^p(X_y,\\mathcal F_y)$. Under these\nconditions the same base-change map is an isomorphism in degree $p-1$.hgraph/nodes/96d74e65854e.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: $\\Hom(X,\\Adual)$ and the $\\ell$-adic Representation\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-thm-serre-torsion-restriction\nlean_status: empty\norder: 156\nref: mumford-abelian-varieties:page-0218\ntitle: Serre torsion restriction\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nFor $n\\ge3$ and ample $L$, the restriction from automorphisms preserving\n$L$ modulo $\\operatorname{Pic}^0X$ to $\\operatorname{Aut}(X_n)$ is injective.hgraph/nodes/b18199c88a51.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Varieties\ncontent_type: lemma\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-lem-abelian-translations\nlean_status: empty\norder: 31\nref: M:ch2:II.4\ntitle: Translations\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nFor every $a\\in X$, the translation $T_a(x)=a+x$ is an automorphism of $X$;\nits inverse is $T_{-a}$.  Consequently, local geometric properties are\npreserved by translation.hgraph/nodes/4366725c7d12.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Varieties\ncontent_type: definition\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-def-abelian-variety\nlean_status: empty\norder: 29\nref: M:ch2:II.4\ntitle: Abelian variety\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nAn abelian variety $X$ is a complete algebraic variety over an algebraically\nclosed field $k$ with a group law $m:X\\times X\\to X$ such that $m$ and the\ninverse map are morphisms of varieties.hgraph/nodes/b36b59890144.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Theorem of Tate (C. P. Ramanujam)\ncontent_type: corollary\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-cor-elliptic-cm-isogenous\nlean_status: empty\norder: 197\nref: mumford-abelian-varieties:page-0269\ntitle: Elliptic curves\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nAny two elliptic curves over finite fields with isomorphic algebras of complex\nmultiplications are isogenous over the algebraic closure. In particular, any\ntwo supersingular elliptic curves are isogenous.hgraph/nodes/50899daa2f53.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Varieties\ncontent_type: definition\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-def-f-flat\nlean_status: empty\norder: 43\nref: M:ch2:II.5\ntitle: Flat over the base\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nA quasicoherent $\\mathcal F$ on $X$ is $f$-flat if every $\\mathcal F_x$ is\n$\\mathcal O_{Y,f(x)}$-flat; equivalently, sections over affine $U$ mapping into\naffine $V$ are flat $\\mathcal O_Y(V)$-modules.hgraph/nodes/6bfa9e5202e2.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Schemes\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-thm-finite-groupscheme-quotient\nlean_status: empty\norder: 87\nref: M:ch3:III.12\ntitle: Quotient by a finite group scheme\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nLet a finite group scheme $G$ act on $X$, with every orbit contained in an\naffine open. There is a quotient $(Y,\\pi)$ whose underlying topological\nspace is the orbit quotient and for which\n$\\mathcal O_Y\\simeq\\pi_*\\mathcal O_X^G$. It is unique, finite and\nsurjective, and has the universal property for invariant morphisms.\nIf the action is free, $\\pi$ is flat of degree $n=\\dim_kR$ for\n$G=\\operatorname{Spec}R$, $G\\times X\\simeq X\\times_YX$, and pullback is an\nequivalence between coherent sheaves on $Y$ and coherent $G$-sheaves on $X$.hgraph/nodes/42cdffe236ec.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Varieties\ncontent_type: proposition\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-source-character-kernel-pic\nlean_status: empty\norder: 64\nref: M:ch2:II.7\ntitle: Characters and descended line bundles\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nIf $G$ acts freely on $X$ and $Y=X/G$, then for characters $\\alpha:G\\to k^*$,\n\\[\n \\widehat G\\simeq\\ker[\\Pic Y\\to\\Pic X],\n\\]\nand if $|G|$ is prime to the characteristic, every coherent sheaf on $Y$ is\na direct summand of $\\pi_*(\\pi^*\\mathcal F)$.hgraph/nodes/8c802a16c024.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Varieties\ncontent_type: lemma\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-source-nonsingular\nlean_status: empty\norder: 33\nref: M:ch2:II.4\ntitle: Everywhere nonsingular\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nAn abelian variety is everywhere nonsingular.hgraph/nodes/1b107d86cee0.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Mordell--Weil theorem (Yuri Manin)\ncontent_type: proposition\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-prop-formal-finite-generation\nlean_status: empty\norder: 201\nref: mumford-abelian-varieties:page-0273\ntitle: Formal finite-generation criterion\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nIf an abelian group $\\Gamma$ has finite $\\Gamma/n\\Gamma$ for every $n>1$ and\nhas a pairing with the preceding two properties, then $\\Gamma$ is finitely generated.hgraph/nodes/41df8bdbc790.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Analytic Theory\ncontent_type: lemma\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-source-hermitian-correspondence\nlean_status: empty\norder: 18\nref: M:ch1:I.2\ntitle: Hermitian forms and invariant alternating forms\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nHermitian forms $H$ on $V$ correspond bijectively to real skew-symmetric forms\n$E$ satisfying $E(ix,iy)=E(x,y)$ by\n\\[\n E(x,y)=\\operatorname{Im}H(x,y),\\qquad H(x,y)=E(ix,y)+iE(x,y).\n\\]hgraph/nodes/e2c23d1445e8.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Schemes\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-thm-high-power-very-ample\nlean_status: empty\norder: 122\nref: M:ch3:III.17\ntitle: Very ampleness of powers\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nFor any ample line bundle $L$ on an abelian variety $X$, $L^n$ is very ample\nif $n>3$.hgraph/nodes/b346ede19d0c.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Varieties\ncontent_type: proposition\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-prop-degree-hilbert\nlean_status: empty\norder: 59\nref: M:ch2:II.6\ntitle: Hilbert-polynomial degree\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nLet $X$ be complete of dimension $g$, $L$ invertible, and $\\mathcal F$ coherent.\nWrite $P_{\\mathcal F}(n)=\\chi(\\mathcal F\\otimes L^n)$ and\n$d_L(\\mathcal F)/g!$ for its leading coefficient.  Then\n\\[\nd_L(\\mathcal F)=\\operatorname{rank}(\\mathcal F)\\deg L.\n\\]\nIf $f:Y\\to X$ is a surjective morphism of complete $g$-folds, then\n\\[\n\\deg f^*L=(\\deg f)\\deg L.\n\\]hgraph/nodes/5fa8185edc82.md\n---\nauthor: sync\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T19:46:10'\ndecl: Mumford.Uniformization.genusTorus_exists_division\ndocstring: Nonzero integer division exists on the genus torus model.\nfile: MumfordLib/Uniformization.lean\ngenerated: lean\nlean_status: lean_ok\ntitle: Mumford.Uniformization.genusTorus_exists_division\ntype: lean\nupdated: '2026-08-27T19:46:10'\n---\ntheorem genusTorus_exists_division (g : ℕ) (x : GenusTorus g) {n : ℤ} (hn : n ≠ 0) :\n    ∃ y : GenusTorus g, n • y = x := by\n  exact ⟨DivisibleBy.div x n, DivisibleBy.div_cancel x hn⟩hgraph/nodes/9d079f8666ed.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Varieties\ncontent_type: proposition\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-source-divisorial-biduality\nlean_status: empty\norder: 71\nref: M:ch2:II.8\ntitle: Characterization of the Poincare correspondence\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nFor equal-dimensional $X,Y$ and a divisorial correspondence $Q$, the\nconditions that $Q|_{\\{x\\}\\times Y}$ be trivial only for $x=0$ and that\n$Q|_{X\\times\\{y\\}}$ be trivial only for $y=0$ are equivalent. When they hold,\n$X\\simeq\\widehat Y$, $Y\\simeq\\widehat X$, and $Q$ is the Poincare bundle.hgraph/nodes/7eb3a3fdf85e.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: $\\Hom(X,\\Adual)$ and the $\\ell$-adic Representation\ncontent_type: definition\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-def-simple-abelian-variety\nlean_status: empty\norder: 129\nref: M:ch4:IV.19\ntitle: Simple abelian variety\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nAn abelian variety is simple if it has no abelian subvariety other than zero\nand itself.hgraph/nodes/171731e9b783.md\n---\nauthor: sync\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:57:48'\ndecl: Mumford.GroupScheme.pointTranslation_symm\nfile: MumfordLib/GroupScheme.lean\ngenerated: lean\nlean_status: lean_ok\ntitle: Mumford.GroupScheme.pointTranslation_symm\ntype: lean\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:57:48'\n---\ntheorem pointTranslation_symm (x y : 𝟙_ C ⟶ G) :\n    (pointTranslation G x y).symm = pointTranslation G y x := by\n  simp [pointTranslation]\n\n@[simp]hgraph/nodes/6f3a4438a9cf.md\n---\nauthor: sync\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T18:47:42'\ndecl: Mumford.productTorus_zsmul_torsion_card\ndocstring: The cardinality formula for nonzero-integer torsion on a finite product\n  torus.\nfile: MumfordLib/Analytic.lean\ngenerated: lean\nlean_status: lean_ok\ntitle: Mumford.productTorus_zsmul_torsion_card\ntype: lean\nupdated: '2026-08-27T18:59:31'\n---\ntheorem productTorus_zsmul_torsion_card {d : Type*} [Fintype d] {n : ℤ} (hn : n ≠ 0) :\n    Nat.card {x : ProductTorus d | n • x = 0} = n.natAbs ^ Fintype.card d := by\n  have hpos : 0 < n.natAbs := Int.natAbs_pos.mpr hn\n  let e : {x : ProductTorus d | n • x = 0} ≃\n      {x : ProductTorus d | n.natAbs • x = 0} :=\n    { toFun := fun x => ⟨x.val, (natAbs_nsmul_eq_zero).2 x.property⟩\n      invFun := fun x => ⟨x.val, (natAbs_nsmul_eq_zero).1 x.property⟩\n      left_inv := by intro x; exact Subtype.ext rfl\n      right_inv := by intro x; exact Subtype.ext rfl }\n  rw [Nat.card_congr e]\n  exact productTorus_torsion_card hpos\n\n/-!\nThe torsion carriers are additive subgroups, so the preceding type\nequivalences can be upgraded to additive equivalences.  This is the group\nlevel form needed for the abstract-group statement in the blueprint.\n-/hgraph/nodes/ef51db0ea795.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Schemes\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-thm-dual-isogeny-kernel\nlean_status: empty\norder: 111\nref: M:ch3:III.15\ntitle: Kernel of the dual isogeny\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nIf $f:X\\to Y$ is an isogeny with kernel $K$ and dual map\n$\\widehat f:\\widehat Y\\to\\widehat X$ with kernel $K'$, then\n$K'\\simeq\\widehat K$ canonically.hgraph/nodes/52abbf743f5b.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Mordell--Weil theorem (Yuri Manin)\ncontent_type: lemma\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-lem-abelian-integral-model\nlean_status: empty\norder: 204\nref: mumford-abelian-varieties:page-0275\ntitle: Integral model\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nFor a ring of integers $A$ of $K$, after shrinking $Y={\\rm Spec}A_s$ there are\na projective $\\widetilde X\\to Y$, multiplication $\\widetilde m$, and section\n$\\widetilde e$ whose generic fibre is $X$, whose closed fibres are abelian\nvarieties, and with $\\widetilde X(Y)\\simeq X(K)$.hgraph/nodes/a5dc7530207b.md\n---\nauthor: sync\nchapter: Algebraic Theory via Schemes\ncontent_type: theorem\ncreated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\ngenerated: blueprint\nlabel: mumford-thm-riemann-roch\nlean_status: empty\norder: 115\nref: M:ch3:III.16\ntitle: Riemann--Roch\ntype: tex\nupdated: '2026-08-27T16:28:25'\n---\nFor $L\\simeq\\mathcal O_X(D)$ on a $g$-dimensional abelian variety,\n\\[\n\\chi(L)=\\frac{(D^g)}{g!},\\qquad \\chi(L)^2=\\deg\\phi_L.\n\\]"}], "subagent_thread_id": "01a04501-7586-73c1-adf5-c755298b0290", "parent_thread_id": "01a044ff-ff1e-7e82-bacc-0c271468e6b9", "agent_path": "/root/cross_api", "agent_nickname": "Carson", "agent_role": "explorer", "depth": 1, "source": "/home/axel/.codex-temp/sessions/2026/08/28/rollout-2026-08-28T04-55-18-01a04501-7586-73c1-adf5-c755298b0290.jsonl", "model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "subagent_type": "explorer", "codex_event_id": "8c549795759157734aabb3d5145798