/home/axel/LeanAlgebraicGeometry-Horizon/.archon-horizon/runs/0180/sessions/0008-horizon-fs-mumford/transcript.jsonl:562:{"kind": "tool_result", "at": "2026-08-27T21:06:34.622000+00:00", "text": "", "tool": "", "data": {"content": [{"type": "input_text", "text": "Script completed\nWall time 0.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"}, {"type": "input_text", "text": "id: I-2048\nprovider: local\nkind: issue\nbody: 'Torsion API proves cardinality, not the stated group isomorphism\n\n\n  `unitAddCircle_torsion_equiv_zmod` and `productTorus_torsion_equiv_pi_zmod` return\n  plain `Equiv`, and the integer bridge/cardinality theorems likewise only identify\n  underlying types. The blueprint proposition `mumford-frag-torsion` claims the annihilator\n  subgroup is isomorphic as an abstract group to `(Z/nZ)^(2g)`. The current subtype\n  is not an additive subgroup, so this is a real API/formalization gap rather than\n  a cosmetic annotation issue. Next action: define the torsion `AddSubgroup` (or provide\n  an `AddEquiv`) and prove the homomorphism laws, then connect the product index to\n  `2 * g`; until then describe these declarations as set bijections/cardinality infrastructure.'\nlabels:\n- agent-ready\nstatus: open\nscope:\n  projects:\n  - Mumford\naudience: horizon\nauthor: horizon\nsource_ref: null\ncreated_at: '2026-08-27T10:50:24.219434+00:00'\nupdated_at: '2026-08-27T15:22:38.158543+00:00'\nmetadata:\n  provenance:\n    run: '0165'\n    session: 0004-horizon-fs-mumford\n    role: horizon\n    round: '1'\n    rounds: '3'\n    task: fs-mumford\n    task_title: Advance Mumford Abelian Varieties formalization\n    projects: Mumford\n  agent: ground\n  read_by:\n  - fs-mumford\n"}, {"type": "input_text", "text": "id: I-2068\nprovider: local\nkind: conversation\nbody: 'Survey handoff from frontier subtask\n\n\n  Current Part02 Lean surface: only 5 modules, all axiom-clean and no sorrys. AffineBasics.lean:27-74\n  wraps membership, D(1)/D(0), nilpotence, product/intersection, openness, basis;\n  80-110 wraps spectrum comap functoriality. AffineOpens.lean:24-31 wraps affine-open\n  basis and iSup cover. OpenImmersion.lean:23-44 wraps injectivity/open map/range/factorization.\n  Schemes.lean:30-126 wraps local-ring/stalk, local affine, openSubscheme, preimage\n  and composition. StandardOpen.lean:26-35 gives localization for affine standard\n  opens; 49-77 gives canonical localization ring map and algebraMap compatibility.\n\n\n  Highest-value concrete next target: Schemes lemma `schemes-lemma-standard-open`\n  (blueprint/src/ch01-schemes.tex:498-575, Tag 01HS), especially finite standard-open\n  refinement of an open cover of D(f), plus unit-ideal criterion. Existing Part01\n  `StacksPart01Lib/Spectrum.lean:103-110` already gives `standardOpen_quasiCompact`;\n  `Zariski.lean:52-56` gives `standardOpen_iSup_eq_top_iff`; Mathlib Prime.Topology\n  has `isCompact_basicOpen` at line 619 and `iSup_basicOpen_eq_top_iff` at 628. A\n  practical Lean API can first add Part02 aliases for these, then prove a finite-index\n  theorem using `IsCompact.elim_finite_subcover` + `standardOpen_isTopologicalBasis`\n  (AffineBasics.lean:70-74). This is likely straightforward and directly unlocks the\n  sheaf-on-standard-opens path.\n\n\n  Second target: schemes-lemma-standard-open-affine (blueprint ch01 lines 962-985,\n  Tag 01I3), but this may already be available as Mathlib `Scheme.isAffine_basicOpen`/affine\n  restriction APIs; inspect before re-proving. Third: schemes-lemma-standard-open-two-affines\n  (ch01 lines 2020-2045, Tag 01IW), mathematically important but substantially harder\n  due affine-open intersection and localization; defer until finite cover API exists.\n\n\n  Blueprint protection: do not edit blueprint. Prior Part02 handoff C-0001 says next\n  frontier finite standard-open refinements, module maps, broader scheme lemmas; recent\n  commits in this project did not add Lean files beyond wrappers. Cross-project reads\n  are allowed, writes only this project.'\nlabels:\n- agent-ready\nstatus: archived\nscope: {}\naudience: task:fs-stacks-part02-schemes\nauthor: horizon\nsource_ref: null\ncreated_at: '2026-08-27T16:23:52.223520+00:00'\nupdated_at: '2026-08-27T20:50:46.466439+00:00'\nmetadata:\n  conversation: true\n  participants:\n  - task:fs-stacks-part02-schemes\n  started_by: task:fs-stacks-part02-schemes\n  read_by:\n  - fs-stacks-part02-schemes\n  provenance:\n    run: 0183\n    session: 0004-horizon-fs-stacks-part02-schemes\n    role: horizon\n    round: '1'\n    rounds: '24'\n    task: fs-stacks-part02-schemes\n    task_title: Advance Stacks Part 02 Schemes formalization\n    projects: StacksPart02_Schemes\n"}, {"type": "input_text", "text": "id: I-0906\nprovider: local\nkind: memory\nbody: 'A CROSS-PROJECT ''PORT IT FROM NEXT DOOR'' CAN BE A NO-OP BECAUSE THE DECLARATION\n  IS ALREADY HOME UNDER THE SAME NAME -- grep your OWN project for the name before\n  pricing the port\n\n\n  Measured AJC run 0084 (ajc-p2), 2026-07-29, on the P5 / extension-uniformity item.\n\n\n  THE SEQUENCE, which is the interesting part: the same item was mis-priced twice\n  in one hour, in OPPOSITE directions, by careful sources.\n\n  1. Too expensive: review-ajc (I-0867) listed P5 UniformVanishing as ''ABSENT AS\n  A MODULE (not started)'' in AJC.\n\n  2. Too cheap: its own correction (I-0904) then said a sorry-free sibling exists\n  in AJCR (RiemannRoch/UniformVanishing.lean:71, exists_bound_subsingleton_hModule_one_of_isFinite_toP1)\n  and the work is ''a quantifier exchange plus a PORT''.\n\n  Both are wrong about the port. AJC ALREADY HAS THAT DECLARATION, same name, same\n  Subsingleton (HModule (divisorSheaf K D) 1) conclusion: Ledger/FiberBound.lean:113,\n  plus the curve-level form with the map-to-P1 and both finiteness binders discharged\n  at :202. There is nothing to port. And AJCR has NO extension-uniform (exists-b-forall-kappa)\n  statement at all -- grep for UniformVanishing/UniformBaseDivisor across AJCR returns\n  zero defs -- so the sibling is not ahead here, it is behind.\n\n\n  THE RULE. I-0529 says a sibling hit means IMPORT-OR-PORT, not PROVE. Add a step\n  before that: grep YOUR OWN project for the name first. A cross-project search naturally\n  surfaces the neighbour''s copy, and if both projects descend from a shared ancestor\n  the name is often identical -- so the neighbour looks like the source and your own\n  copy never appears in the result you acted on. The ''port'' then costs a session\n  and lands a duplicate.\n\n\n  SECOND RULE, and it is what actually moved this item. Neither pricing was about\n  the real gap. AJC''s own upstream docstring (ExtensionUniformity.lean:352-355) had\n  the correct diagnosis all along -- a D0 exists per field, only degree control is\n  missing -- while the DOWNSTREAM index (GenusFieldInvariance.lean:426-438) said the\n  residue was a missing production from geometry confined to genus 0, and the index\n  is what a lane reads first. When two docstrings about one gap disagree, the one\n  nearer the DEFINITION tends to be right and the one nearer the CONSUMER tends to\n  have drifted. Landed the split at every genus in 71f1cf403; see I-0897.'\nlabels:\n- agent-ready\nstatus: archived\nscope: {}\naudience: horizon\nauthor: horizon\nsource_ref: null\ncreated_at: '2026-07-29T12:11:23.132675+00:00'\nupdated_at: '2026-07-30T04:16:14.344119+00:00'\nmetadata:\n  provenance:\n    run: 0084\n    session: 0002-horizon-ajc-p2\n    role: horizon\n    round: '0'\n    rounds: '8'\n    task: ajc-p2\n    task_title: 'AJC prover p2: claim and close the 2nd-most important representability\n      item'\n    projects: Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge\n  read_by:\n  - ajc-p2\n"}, {"type": "input_text", "text": "id: I-2035\nprovider: local\nkind: protection\nbody: '[persistent] FS cross-project read, single-project write\n\n\n  Each FormalizedSources formalization task may read all registered projects (especially\n  every path under FormalizedSources/, plus MainProjects) to adapt definitions, lemmas,\n  and API shape. It may write only its own project''s Lean library and minimal blueprint\n  corrections. Do not edit other projects'' Lean or blueprints. Prefer horizon search\n  and sibling formalizations before inventing parallel infrastructure.'\nlabels:\n- agent-ready\nstatus: open\nscope: {}\naudience: horizon\nauthor: human\nsource_ref: null\ncreated_at: '2026-08-27T08:27:30.090374+00:00'\nupdated_at: '2026-08-27T15:09:54.668439+00:00'\nmetadata:\n  read_by:\n  - ajcr-finite-stage-api-redesign\n  - fs-stacks-part08-modulitheory\n  - fs-mumford\n  - fs-hartshorne\n  - fs-milne\n  - fs-stacks-part01-prelim\n  - fs-stacks-part06-deform\n  - fs-stacks-part04-alg-spaces\n  - fs-stacks-part03-scheme-topicstheory\n  - fs-stacks-part07-alg-stacks\n  - fs-stacks-part05-geom-topics\n  - fs-stacks-part02-schemes\n"}], "subagent_thread_id": "01a04501-7586-73c1-adf5-c755298b0290", "parent_thread_id": "01a044ff-ff1e-7e82-bacc-0c271468e6b9", "agent_path": "/root/cross_api", "agent_nickname": "Carson", "agent_role": "explorer", "depth": 1, "source": "/home/axel/.codex-temp/sessions/2026/08/28/rollout-2026-08-28T04-55-18-01a04501-7586-73c1-adf5-c755298b0290.jsonl", "model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "subagent_type": "explorer", "codex_event_id": "9c117900e2b9ebfe2359d469c020c06835affaca77d77dd23344de9dae621244", "codex_source": "/home/axel/.codex-temp/sessions/2026/08/28/rollout-2026-08-28T04-55-18-01a04501-7586-73c1-adf5-c755298b0290.jsonl"}, "usage": null}
